<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramón Matías Mella</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramón Matías Mella was a Dominican patriot, counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Juan Pablo Duarte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-del-rosario-sanchez">Francisco del Rosario Sánchez</a> as one of the three founding fathers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. On 27 February 1844 he fired the shot that signalled the uprising against Haitian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. He later fought in the war of independence and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> annexed the country in 1861, joined the Restoration War, dying in 1864.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-matias-mella.jpg" alt="Ramón Matías Mella" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: General de San Juan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_de_Historia_y_Geograf%C3%ADa_-_Retrato_del_General_Mella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mella was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on 25 February 1816, during the period known as the España Boba, to Antonio and Francisca, of Spanish descent; his father was a merchant. Little is recorded of his schooling, which was probably private. As a young man he was said to be skilled with sword and sabre, and at 19 he began working as a cutter of precious woods in San Cristóbal. His name is debated: most documents of his lifetime, including his baptism and marriage records, call him Ramón Mella Castillo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1838 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-trinitaria">La Trinitaria</a>, the secret society <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Juan Pablo Duarte</a> founded to win independence from Haitian rule, and became one of Duarte's closest collaborators. On the night of 27 February 1844, when fewer conspirators gathered than expected and some proposed delay, he refused to turn back and fired his blunderbuss into the air at the Puerta de la Misericordia. He then sat on the Central Government Board and went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> to organise the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-santana">Pedro Santana</a>'s coup of July 1844 his position weakened and he went into exile, returning under the amnesty of President Manuel Jimenes in 1848. He held a command at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-carreras">Battle of Las Carreras</a> in 1849, and from December 1853 led a diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> seeking a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> or at least recognition. In the revolution against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenaventura-baez">Buenaventura Báez</a> that began in 1857 he directed the seizure of Samaná in May 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the country was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 20 March 1861, Mella refused allegiance, was held in the Ozama Fortress and deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>, where he lived in poverty. He returned in secret, joined the provisional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> as Minister of War and in January 1864 issued a manual of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare suited to the country's terrain. Ill, he died in Santiago on 4 June 1864, having asked to be buried in the national flag. In 1894 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulises-heureaux">Ulises Heureaux</a> recognised Duarte, Mella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-del-rosario-sanchez">Francisco del Rosario Sánchez</a> as the Founding Fathers, and since 1944 their ashes have lain at the Altar of the Homeland at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerta-del-conde">Puerta del Conde</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matías Ramón Mella” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 27 February 1844 Francisco del Rosario Sánchez and <b>Ramón Matías Mella</b> proclaimed independence at the Puerta del Conde in Santo Domingo.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-dominican-republic">First Dominican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. Died in Santiago de los Caballeros, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.46,-69.94&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Santo Domingo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ramón Matías Mella"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4198 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4198 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.46,-69.94&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
